                <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
                    Frequently Asked Questions
                </h2>

                <div class="space-y-6">
                    <div>
                        <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use regular play sand instead of paver sand as a cheap alternative?</h4>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">No. Play sand has rounded, smooth grains that cannot interlock under compaction. It will wash out of joints rapidly, allow pavers to shift, and provide zero stability. Always use angular paver sand (ASTM C-33) or one of the alternatives listed above. The cost difference between play sand and proper paver sand is minimal, but the performance difference is enormous. See our <a href="/paver-sand-vs-play-sand" class="text-[#1E90FF] hover:underline">paver sand vs play sand comparison</a> for details.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I mix polymeric sand with regular sand to save money?</h4>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">This is not recommended. The polymer binder concentration in polymeric sand is precisely calibrated by the manufacturer for optimal bonding. Diluting it with regular sand reduces the polymer-to-sand ratio below the effective threshold, resulting in joints that partially bond but remain weak enough to fail under normal conditions. You end up spending money on polymeric sand without getting its benefits. Either use polymeric sand at full concentration or choose one of the dedicated alternatives listed above.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is stabilized decomposed granite the same as polymeric sand?</h4>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">No, though they share some similarities. Stabilized DG uses a natural resin binder mixed with decomposed granite particles. It produces a firm, semi-hardened surface that is softer and more flexible than cured polymeric sand. Stabilized DG is typically used as a pathway surfacing material rather than a paver joint fill, though it can work in wider joints (1/2 inch or more). It does not achieve the rigid locking bond of polymeric sand and provides less weed prevention.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does EnviroSAND last compared to polymeric sand?</h4>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">EnviroSAND typically lasts 3 to 5 years before needing reapplication, compared to 5 to 10 years for premium synthetic polymeric sands. The shorter lifespan is due to the organic binder's natural biodegradation — which is actually a feature rather than a bug from an environmental perspective. The trade-off is more frequent reapplication in exchange for zero microplastic pollution. Reapplication is straightforward: sweep in fresh EnviroSAND and reactivate with water.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I apply a paver joint sealer over existing polymeric sand?</h4>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">Yes, this is actually a common professional practice. Applying a joint-stabilizing sealer over existing polymeric sand joints adds an additional layer of protection and extends the life of the polymeric sand beneath. The liquid sealer penetrates into the top portion of the polymeric sand, reinforcing its surface and adding UV protection. This combination provides the longest-lasting joint stabilization available.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What about using mortar between pavers instead of sand?</h4>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">Traditional mortar (Portland cement plus sand) creates extremely rigid joints that cannot flex with natural ground movement. On interlocking paver installations (which are designed as flexible systems), rigid mortar joints will crack and crumble within one to three freeze-thaw seasons. Mortar is appropriate for pavers that are installed on a concrete slab (like a thin-set paver overlay), but it should never be used as a jointing material on standard sand-set paver installations.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is there a DIY polymeric sand I can make at home?</h4>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">Some online tutorials suggest mixing regular sand with Portland cement or clear PVA glue to create a homemade polymeric sand. While these mixtures do harden, they are significantly inferior to commercial products. Cement mixtures are too rigid and will crack. Glue mixtures produce inconsistent bonding and break down rapidly when exposed to UV and moisture. The cost savings are minimal, and the performance gap is large. Commercial polymeric sand or the alternatives listed above are worth the modest investment over DIY experiments.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

